<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>\mathbf{p} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ \displaystyle{10} \\ \displaystyle{2} \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{-5} \\ y \\ \displaystyle{1} \end{pmatrix}</m> are parallel, with <m>\mathbf{p} = k\mathbf{q}</m>. Fractions or decimals are fine.</p><div>(a) <m>k =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/>  </div><div>(b) <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/>  </div><div>(c) <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Every component of <m>\mathbf{p}</m> is <m>k</m> times the matching component of <m>\mathbf{q}</m>.</p><p>(a) The bottom components are both known: <m>{2} = k \times ({1})</m>, so <m>k = {2}</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>x = k \times ({-5}) = {-10}</m>.</p><p>(c) <m>{10} = k\,y</m>, so <m>y = {10} \div k = {5}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>2</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-10</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>